(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-exam-2-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient taking colestipol also takes thyroxine. How should the thyroxine be timed?","choices":["At the same time as the resin","30 minutes after the resin","Timing does not matter","1 hour before or 4 hours after"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Resins interfere with absorption of digoxin, warfarin, thyroxine, beta blockers and thiazide diuretics; giving the drug 1 hour before or 4 hours after the resin avoids it. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has three hours of chest pressure. The electrocardiogram shows ST-segment depression, and the cardiac biomarker is elevated. He has no bleeding history. Which therapy is not recommended for him?","choices":["Aspirin","Reteplase","Metoprolol","Enoxaparin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrinolytics are not recommended in non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome, because the bleeding risk outweighs the benefit. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which beta blocker also blocks alpha-1 receptors?","choices":["Metoprolol","Carvedilol","Atenolol","Propranolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carvedilol and labetalol are the third-generation exceptions that add alpha-1 blockade; carvedilol is also antioxidant and antiproliferative. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is characteristic of oral minoxidil?","choices":["Hypertrichosis","Lupus syndrome","Cyanide toxicity","Dry cough"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oral minoxidil causes hair growth on the face, back, arms and legs; it also causes myocardial ischemia and arrhythmias. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which substance is listed as a niacin interaction?","choices":["Antacids","Warfarin","Alcohol","Thyroxine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Niacin interactions are statins, bile acid sequestrants and alcohol. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How do angiotensin receptor blockers lower blood pressure?","choices":["By blocking AT1 receptors","By inhibiting renin release","By inhibiting the converting enzyme","By stimulating central alpha-2 sites"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They bind the AT1 (angiotensin II type 1) receptor with high affinity and dissociate slowly, giving sustained blockade: vasodilation, less aldosterone and less sodium reabsorption. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"When a non-selective beta blocker is first started, what happens to total peripheral resistance?","choices":["It falls immediately","It rises by reflex","It does not change","It falls, then rises"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac output falls, so peripheral resistance rises reflexly at first; mean arterial pressure falls only with chronic therapy as the baroreflex adjusts. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Why is a statin combined with a fibrate only with caution?","choices":["Loss of LDL lowering","Higher risk of flushing","Higher risk of hyperkalemia","Higher risk of myopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both classes can cause myopathy, so they are combined cautiously; gemfibrozil is a relative contraindication with statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which non-infectious causes of conjunctivitis are commoner than the bacterial ones?","choices":["Viruses, allergy, irritants and contact lenses","Only bacterial pathogens are listed as common","Tumors and systemic disease","Fungal keratitis and herpes zoster"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These are given as the common causes, with immune-mediated reactions, systemic disease and tumors as less common. Bacterial pathogens come after — a reminder that most red eyes are not bacterial. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which factors determine whether an ocular infection is treated topically or with oral or parenteral agents?","choices":["The organism's Gram stain alone","The patient's visual acuity alone","Whether the patient wears contact lenses","Setting, age, immune status and extent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Those four decide the route. Preseptal disease may be manageable topically or orally, while postseptal or orbital cellulitis is a different proposition entirely. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"In which patient is hydralazine contraindicated?","choices":["A young adult with severe hypertension","A patient already taking a diuretic","A patient already on a beta blocker","An older adult with coronary disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hydralazine is contraindicated in coronary artery disease, the elderly and ischemia, because vasodilation with reflex sympathetic activation raises cardiac workload. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What congestion is pseudoephedrine indicated to relieve?","choices":["Nasal and sinus congestion from a cold","Congestion from bacterial sinusitis only","Chest congestion with productive cough","Congestion from nasal polyps only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It covers both infective and allergic causes, because it acts on the swollen vessels rather than on the cause of the swelling. Hay fever or allergy. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Why does stopping a beta blocker abruptly provoke a rebound?","choices":["Catecholamine synthesis stops","Renin release is permanently lost","Receptors upregulate during blockade","The drug has a long half-life"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic blockade upregulates beta receptors, so sudden removal exposes more receptors to catecholamines; the drug is withdrawn slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"How does pseudoephedrine differ from oxymetazoline in mechanism and route?","choices":["It blocks histamine receptors and is taken orally","Same alpha agonist mechanism, but taken orally","It is an alpha antagonist given orally","It is a topical steroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The mechanism is shared; taking it orally is what changes both its usefulness and its side effect profile. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What non-antibiotic measure is described as useful in bacterial rhinosinusitis?","choices":["Oral antihistamines","Saline irrigation","Systemic corticosteroids","Topical decongestant for ten days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the one adjunct named, and it costs nothing in resistance. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which otic ingredient is singled out for causing hypersensitivity?","choices":["Hydrocortisone","Neomycin","Ciprofloxacin","Ofloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A patient whose ear gets worse rather than better on drops may be reacting to the neomycin rather than failing treatment. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A patient takes clonidine together with a beta blocker. What happens to blood pressure if the clonidine is stopped abruptly?","choices":["It falls sharply","It stays the same","It rises sharply","It rises only slightly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. While both are taken they lower pressure together, but abrupt clonidine withdrawal in this combination produces a dramatic rise in blood pressure. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is indicated for heart failure?","choices":["Propranolol","Atenolol","Bisoprolol","Nadolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The three beta blockers used for heart failure are carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and bisoprolol. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which statement describes the place of bile acid sequestrants in therapy?","choices":["Most efficacious and best tolerated","First choice when triglycerides are over 1000 mg/dL","Injectable only and expensive","Safest, but poorly tolerated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They are the safest because they have no systemic effects, but poorly tolerated; they are used with a statin or where only modest LDL (low-density lipoprotein) reductions are needed. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A patient takes aspirin for fever. When are they told to come back straight away?","choices":["If they feel drowsy after the first dose","Only once a full week has passed","If the fever returns at any point","If the fever does not come down"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The instruction is about the fever failing to respond, which is a different signal from the fever simply continuing. That is, if it fails to respond to the medicine rather than merely persisting. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which form of niacin is NOT effective as an antilipemic?","choices":["Nicotinic acid","Extended-release niacin","Niacinamide","Immediate-release niacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Niacin is a B-complex vitamin; nicotinic acid is the antilipemic form, and its amide, niacinamide (nicotinamide), is not effective. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How do antihistamines reduce nasal and bronchial secretions during an allergic reaction?","choices":["Through their antimuscarinic action","By blocking H2 receptors on glands","By constricting the vessels supplying the mucosa","By thinning the mucus already produced"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The drying is a muscarinic effect rather than a histamine one, which is why the second generation agents dry you out less. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which agents are listed as available otic anti-infectives?","choices":["Amoxicillin, azithromycin and cefdinir, as drops","Gentamicin and tobramycin, given as ear drops","Ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin, and two steroid combinations","Nystatin and ketoconazole, given as ear drops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two plain fluoroquinolones and two combinations, and the combinations are where the restrictions live. In full: ciprofloxacin, ciprofloxacin with dexamethasone, neomycin with polymyxin B and hydrocortisone, and ofloxacin. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How should ezetimibe be taken in relation to meals?","choices":["With or without meals","Within 1 hour of a meal","Only on an empty stomach","Only with a high-fat meal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe can be taken with or without meals, unlike bile acid sequestrants, which are taken within 1 hour of a meal. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which commonly used product lowers ezetimibe concentrations?","choices":["Antacids","Grapefruit juice","Alcohol","Salt substitutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antacids decrease ezetimibe concentrations, as bile acid sequestrants may; cyclosporine increases them. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which drug is taken every day to prevent anginal attacks rather than to relieve an acute one?","choices":["Sublingual nitroglycerin","Nitroglycerin lingual spray","Aspirin","Metoprolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers, calcium channel blockers and long-acting nitrates are taken to prevent attacks; they are not rescue drugs. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which organisms are named as the bacterial causes of acute otitis media?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus, Pseudomonas and Proteus","Group A Streptococcus and Epstein-Barr virus","Candida and Aspergillus","Streptococcus pneumoniae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The same three run through otitis media and bacterial sinusitis, which is why the antibiotic reasoning for the two is so similar. Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has used an over-the-counter redness-relief drop every day for a month. His eyes are redder than when he started. What has happened?","choices":["He has bacterial conjunctivitis","The drops have raised his intraocular pressure","Rebound hyperemia","He has developed allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beyond about two weeks, stopping leaves the eye redder than it started. This is the counseling point most worth carrying out of this topic. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which calcium channel blocker would be used to slow the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation?","choices":["Nicardipine","Nimodipine","Isradipine","Diltiazem"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem strongly suppresses the sinoatrial node and atrioventricular conduction; atrial fibrillation or flutter is an approved non-dihydropyridine indication. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 26-year-old finds his vision blurs badly on pilocarpine. Why is this class particularly poorly tolerated in younger patients?","choices":["It causes iris color change in the young","Younger patients absorb more drug systemically","Miosis blurs vision","The drug raises intraocular pressure in the young"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — fixed small pupils, myopia and visual disturbance, and compliance suffers for it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"When is an angiotensin receptor blocker used for left ventricular dysfunction?","choices":["When an ACE inhibitor is not tolerated","Only after a beta blocker fails","Only with a potassium supplement","Never, as it worsens heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor is the default in left ventricular dysfunction; the receptor blocker is the replacement when the patient cannot tolerate it. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has had crushing chest pressure with sweating for 30 minutes, and the ambulance crew records ST-segment elevation. He has no allergy and no bleeding history. Which drug reduces mortality and reinfarction and is given first?","choices":["Sublingual nitroglycerin","Chewed aspirin","Oral metoprolol","Intravenous morphine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is given at the first signs of chest pain, chewed and swallowed, and it reduces mortality and reinfarction. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which antibiotic is a relative contraindication with statins?","choices":["Amoxicillin","Cephalexin","Doxycycline","Erythromycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Erythromycin is on the relative contraindication list with cyclosporine and other immunosuppressants, gemfibrozil and niacin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"In the traditional comorbidity-based approach, which drug type is avoided for angina in a patient with diabetes?","choices":["Cardioselective beta blockers","Long-acting nitrates","Non-cardioselective beta blockers","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-cardioselective beta blockers are the type avoided in diabetes, because beta blockers can raise blood sugar and mask the warning signs of hypoglycemia; a cardioselective one is the listed alternative. Current practice is less absolute: beta blockers are not contraindicated in diabetes, but a cardioselective one is preferred and the patient is taught about masked hypoglycemia. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A patient wants to stop his metoprolol abruptly before a trip. What is the danger?","choices":["Hypoglycemia, sweating or confusion","Bronchospasm, wheeze or cough","Angina, infarction or a pressure surge","Hyperkalemia, weakness or palpitations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sudden withdrawal can cause acute angina, myocardial infarction or a marked rise in blood pressure, so the drug is withdrawn slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which nuclear transcription factor do fibrates activate?","choices":["The LDL receptor","HMG-CoA reductase","PPAR-alpha","PCSK9"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates activate PPAR-alpha, increasing fatty acid oxidation (so less VLDL, very low-density lipoprotein, is secreted) and increasing ApoA-1 expression. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What distinguishes naproxen from ibuprofen?","choices":["It is selective for COX-2","A longer half-life","It has no gastrointestinal effects","It is available only on prescription"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Same class and same problems, but a longer half-life, which matters for adherence more than for efficacy. So it is taken less often. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 58-year-old with asthma needs a beta blocker drop for glaucoma. Which agent, and why?","choices":["Levobunolol, because it is well tolerated","Carteolol, because it is beta-2 selective","Timolol, because it is more efficacious","Betaxolol — beta-1 selective"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The nonselective agents are more efficacious in the eye but carry the respiratory risk. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What adverse effects are described with oral nystatin?","choices":["QTc prolongation and hepatitis","Sedation and dry mouth","Nosebleed and perforation of the septum","Diarrhea, nausea, stomach pain and vomiting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All gastrointestinal, which is exactly what you would predict for a drug that stays in the gut and is never absorbed. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which classes reduce aqueous PRODUCTION?","choices":["Prostaglandins, beta blockers and cholinergic agonists","Cholinergic agonists and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors only","Prostaglandins alone","Alpha agonists, beta blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The alpha agonists sit on both sides of the list, which is the part most easily got wrong — they reduce production AND increase outflow. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Besides suppressing inflammation, what other effect of glucocorticoids is useful in the eye?","choices":["They inhibit fibrin and collagen deposition, reducing scar formation","They increase aqueous outflow, lowering pressure","They promote corneal epithelial healing","They have antimicrobial activity against surface organisms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a structure where a scar in the wrong place costs vision, limiting collagen deposition is a benefit in its own right — and it is part of why they are used after surgery. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A patient has paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia. Which calcium channel blocker is appropriate?","choices":["Amlodipine","Nifedipine","Felodipine","Verapamil"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Supraventricular tachycardia -- atrial fibrillation or flutter and paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia -- is an approved indication for the non-dihydropyridines. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How is a nitroglycerin patch or ointment scheduled through the day?","choices":["12 hours on, then 12 hours off","Worn continuously, changed daily","Applied only when pain starts","6 hours on, then 18 hours off"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The patch or ointment is worn for 12 hours and left off for 12 hours each day. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is notable about natamycin among the ophthalmic antifungals?","choices":["It is the only antifungal effective against Fusarium","It is the only antifungal given intravitreally","It is the only commercially available ophthalmic antifungal","It is the only antifungal free of ocular irritation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Everything else on the antifungal table — amphotericin B, the imidazoles — is given by another route or has to be compounded. Availability is why natamycin is the one to know by name. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How does inhibiting hepatic cholesterol synthesis lower serum LDL (low-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["It blocks intestinal cholesterol uptake","It upregulates hepatic LDL receptors","It binds bile acids in the gut","It activates PPAR-alpha"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lower intracellular cholesterol stimulates LDL receptor synthesis, increasing hepatic uptake of LDL, VLDL remnants and IDL from the circulation. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why are beta blockers contraindicated in a patient with COPD (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease) who has bronchospasm?","choices":["They cause bradykinin-induced cough","They block airway beta-2 receptors","They cause sodium and water retention","They raise blood carbon dioxide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blocking pulmonary beta-2 receptors reduces bronchial smooth muscle relaxation; about a third of COPD patients develop bronchospasm. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A patient with high triglycerides has existing gallbladder disease. Which agent should be avoided?","choices":["Niacin","Fenofibrate","Atorvastatin","Evolocumab"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Existing gallbladder disease contraindicates fibrates, which cause cholelithiasis. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"For which patients are ointments preferred over drops, and what must they be warned about?","choices":["Children and poor compliers; vision blurs for twenty minutes","Contact lens wearers, warned to keep lenses in to hold the ointment in place","Adults with glaucoma, warned that ointment raises pressure","Patients with dry eye, warned that ointment worsens tear film stability"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ointment works even if it only reaches the lashes, because it still makes contact with the eye — which is exactly why it suits a squirming child. The trade-off is blurring, so no driving until it clears. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Why is amoxicillin given at a high dose rather than a standard one in acute otitis media?","choices":["It shortens the course to a single day","It penetrates the middle ear only at high concentrations","It overcomes Streptococcus pneumoniae resistance","It covers Pseudomonas, which standard doses miss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pneumococcal resistance here is a matter of degree rather than all-or-nothing, so raising the concentration restores activity. That is the whole argument for the higher dose. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the shared mechanism of all fibrinolytics?","choices":["Converting plasminogen to plasmin","Inhibiting thromboxane A2 synthesis","Neutralizing activated factor X","Blocking adenosine diphosphate receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All fibrinolytics act directly or indirectly to convert plasminogen to plasmin, which lyses the fibrin clot. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of ocular antihistamines is worth mentioning to a patient who already has dry eyes?","choices":["Increased ocular dryness","Raised intraocular pressure","Permanent iris color change","Corneal ulceration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Along with irritation and headache, antihistamines can worsen dryness — which matters when dry eye is the background problem, since the treatment can aggravate the complaint. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which finding marks the most advanced stage of Reye's syndrome?","choices":["Cerebral edema with coma","Fatty liver with encephalitis","Seizures and multiple organ failure","Vomiting with lethargy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The staging runs from a nonspecific febrile illness to multi-organ failure, which is why the early stage matters so much. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why are bile acid sequestrants approved for children, adolescents and pregnancy?","choices":["They are not absorbed from the gut","They are cleared quickly by the kidney","They have no effect on other drugs","They have no gastrointestinal effects"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because resins stay in the gastrointestinal tract, systemic side effects are limited; they are called the safest drug for that reason. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How do ophthalmic beta blockers lower intraocular pressure?","choices":["They increase outflow through the trabecular meshwork","They block beta receptors in the ciliary epithelium","They inhibit carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary epithelium","They contract the ciliary muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Less catecholamine activation means less cyclic AMP, and less aqueous is made. They act on the production side, not on drainage. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Besides Pseudomonas cover, what is the other indication for which fluoroquinolones are specifically preferred?","choices":["Blepharitis","Ophthalmia neonatorum prophylaxis","Fungal keratitis","Corneal ulcers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Corneal ulcers and suspected Pseudomonas are the two the fluoroquinolones are named for. Against that sit the cost and the emerging resistance, which is why they are not first for everything. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which musculoskeletal harms are described with systemic corticosteroids?","choices":["Tendon rupture and long bone fracture","Muscle cramps and myalgia only","Joint effusions and stiffness","Gout and joint crystal deposition"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Structural damage rather than discomfort, and it can appear without warning. — ENT Jax Pharmacology.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What do tear substitutes contain, and what is the purpose of the thickeners in them?","choices":["Only sterile water, the thickeners preventing evaporation","Electrolytes, surfactants and thickeners","Antibiotic and thickener, to prevent secondary infection","Cyclosporine in a thickened base, to reduce inflammation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Same principle as a gel or ointment — the longer the preparation stays in contact, the more benefit it gives. Balanced salt solution, carboxymethylcellulose and polyvinyl alcohol are the over-the-counter examples. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman asks how long she can keep using her redness-relief drops. What do you tell her?","choices":["As long as her eyes stay red","No more than two weeks","No more than six weeks","No more than 72 hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and if there is no improvement within 72 hours she should stop and be seen. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which condition favors choosing a calcium channel blocker over a beta blocker?","choices":["Prior myocardial infarction","Hypertension with angina","Vasospastic angina","Anxiety with angina"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Calcium channel blockers relieve vasospasm, while beta blockers may worsen vasospastic angina. — Myocardial Ischemia Drugs.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which organ dysfunction, when severe, contraindicates a fibrate?","choices":["Pulmonary or cardiac","Hepatic or renal","Thyroid or adrenal","Pancreatic or splenic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severe hepatic or renal dysfunction contraindicates fibrates, along with pregnancy and existing gallbladder disease. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
